    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <details><summary>How many levels are in Arrows Puzzle Escape?</summary><p>Per the official listing, Arrows – Puzzle Escape contains thousands of handcrafted levels. The developer has not published an exact total, and daily challenges keep adding fresh boards, so any specific number you see online is a guess — treat the official "thousands" as the real answer.</p></details>

    <details><summary>What happens when I lose all hearts in Arrows Puzzle Escape?</summary><p>The developer has not published a detailed statement of what happens when your three hearts run out. What is certain: a collision only happens when you tap an arrow whose path is blocked, and levels have no timer, so you are never forced into a risky tap. Keep hearts by reading the board first — the framework on this page is built exactly around that.</p></details>

    <details><summary>What are the difficulty tiers in Arrows Puzzle Escape?</summary><p>The official difficulty ladder is Normal, Hard, Super Hard, and Nightmare, plus daily challenges that rotate fresh boards. There is no published level-number-to-tier mapping, so judge a tier by its boards: longer paths, denser arrow traffic, and more re-blocking as you clear.</p></details>

    <details><summary>Does Arrows Puzzle Escape have a timer?</summary><p>No. Every level is untimed — the only pressure is your three hearts. You can stare at a board as long as you need, which makes careful planning strictly better than fast tapping.</p></details>

    <details><summary>Does Arrows Puzzle Escape have hints?</summary><p>Yes, Arrows – Puzzle Escape has a built-in hint system. On a tangled board, a hint beats a gamble: collisions cost hearts, hints do not.</p></details>

    <details><summary>What is the fastest way to clear an Arrows Puzzle Escape board?</summary><p>Read the board in layers, tap the free arrows first, and peel each layer off one by one. In practice: slide every arrow with an empty path immediately, clear the outer rim before the interior, then resolve single-blocker pairs — arrows stopped by exactly one other arrow — before anything else. No-timer levels reward that steady peel over speed.</p></details>

